00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.540
That average home bill the opener named is up from $897 for water, sewer, garbage, and parks.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.920
Sury Mayor-elect Doug McCallum wants Chair Mike Hurley to scrap the October 30th sitting

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.760
until the new mayors are sworn in.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.400
He says October 17th brought a new mayor in 13 of those 21 cities, including Vancouver,

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.660
Sury, Richmond, Coquitlam, Delta, and both Langley's.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.080
His reason is the calendar.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.440
The financial plan does not have to be adopted until March, so the November 27th meeting would

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.480
do, unless one item truly cannot wait four weeks.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.380
New Westminster Mayor-elect Daniel Fontaine backs him and says about two-thirds of 41 directors

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.880
were defeated, quit, or did not run. Fontaine's case is accountability. People who will not

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.720
answer for the vote should not lock in a plan the next board may carry for four years,

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:39.500
and he says there is no statutory Friday deadline. He also wants an accountability charter, already

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:44.640
supported by West Vancouver Mayor-elect Kirk Lapointe and White Rock Mayor-elect Chris Shields,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.960
including a review of North Shore Wastewater plant overruns.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.480
That charter was drafted with former Richmond counselor Cash Head.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.080
A Metro Vancouver spokesperson said Monday the vote stays Friday,

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.600
because in October decision lets member cities write their own plans

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.900
and meet the rules on when budgets must be set.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.260
Those meetings, the spokesperson said,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.560
land on the last Friday of the month no matter the election cycle.

